From ce3c39c7c6af8361a2e10560898390631e9392d3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stew Forster <stew675@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 18:18:57 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 10/13] ggml-cuda : barrier-free warp quantize for fp8 staging,
 pp512 6630 -> 6818

The old quantize_fp8 was latency/barrier-bound (1 float per thread, 2
syncthreads per CTA): ~256 GB/s of the ~3 TB/s DRAM budget. The new
quantize_fp8_warp<4> is one warp per (token, 128-col block) with a pure
warp-butterfly amax reduce (no smem, no barriers) and 4 k-blocks per warp
for load ILP; the fp8 encode uses the gfx12 v_cvt_pk_fp8_f32, verified
bit-exact vs the software encoder for all finite inputs. Staging output is
bit-exact, so the wmma/gemv/scalar consumers are untouched.

Quantize on pp512 grids 25.4 -> 12.6 us/launch (-50%), pp512 6630 ->
6818-6841 (+3%), tg64 unchanged, PPL 6.2677 exact, 47/47 GDN tests.

The originally-planned fusion (quantize in the wmma staging) was measured
and abandoned: it wins only on small under-occupied shapes and loses on the
big GEMMs that dominate pp512 (net wash). The warp rewrite gets the same
win with zero risk to the GEMM path.

Assisted-by: Claude Sonnet
